{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target claims a 'covering principle of mutual exclusion at the frontier' guarantees that any candidate surviving the soundness critic is 'necessarily' pushed below the frontier by the relevance critic. But mutual exclusion of verdicts alone does not entail universal coverage of the candidate space: there could be candidates that fail BOTH critics (soundness flags them AND relevance flags them), which is consistent with mutual exclusion of the critics' domains yet breaks the claimed 'any candidate that survives soundness is necessarily rejected by relevance' chain. The argument conflates mutual exclusion of critic roles with exhaustive partition of all candidate outcomes, smuggling in an unsupported completeness premise.","premise":"Mutual exclusion between two critics means their verdict domains do not overlap; it does not imply that every candidate must fall into exactly one critic's rejection set.","counterexample":["Consider a candidate C that violates both a soundness constraint and a relevance constraint. The critics' domains are mutually exclusive (no single constraint belongs to both critics), yet C is flagged by both, and another candidate D might survive soundness yet also survive relevance if relevance's scope does not cover D's particular defect. Mutual exclusion alone cannot rule out D."]}]}